            {
              "id": "6a64a464-8797-4a8f-9230-bb5b643522fa",
              "type": "paragraph",
              "html": "<em><strong>Shakespearean Neuroplay: Reinvigorating the Study of Dramatic Texts &amp; Performance through Cognitive Science </strong></em>was written by Amy Cook, published by Palgrave Macmillan, October 15, 2010."
            },
            {
              "id": "109a2254-d138-436e-ab1b-38200be7f1f5",
              "type": "paragraph",
              "html": ""
            },
            {
              "id": "8b324bee-1c0a-4dac-92fe-aafeb0ee4457",
              "type": "quote",
              "html": "<em>Using Shakespeare's Hamlet as a test subject and cognitive linguistic theory of conceptual blending as a tool, Cook unravels the 'mirror held up to nature' at the center of Shakespeare's play and provides a methodology for applying cognitive science to the study of drama.</em>"
            }
          ]
